1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,940
The phone isn't the expensive part. The plan is.

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:05,820
Want proof? Let's count it in hours of your life.

3
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:09,780
Say you make the middle American wage, about twenty-four fifty-one an hour.

4
00:00:10,260 --> 00:00:12,240
Half of workers make more, half make less.

5
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:15,400
Okay, the phone. Say it costs a thousand bucks.

6
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:19,360
That's about forty-one hours of work, so a full work week and change.

7
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:23,160
Now the plan. Say seventy bucks a month, for three years.

8
00:00:23,660 --> 00:00:25,840
Smash like if your plan is even higher than that.

9
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:31,180
That's two thousand five hundred twenty dollars. Two— no, two and a half phones.

10
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:35,140
In hours? About a hundred and three. Just to keep the thing turned on.

11
00:00:35,580 --> 00:00:38,100
Send this to the friend who still brags about their new phone.

12
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:40,160
So, phone plus plan is three thousand five hundred twenty dollars.

13
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:45,300
Wait, that's almost a hundred bucks a month, all in.

14
00:00:45,820 --> 00:00:48,620
Follow if you want more stuff priced in your actual hours.

15
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:53,540
Total time? A hundred and forty-four hours. That's eighteen full work days.

16
00:00:53,960 --> 00:00:57,460
And if a fifth of your check goes to taxes, it's closer to twenty-two days.

17
00:00:57,460 --> 00:01:01,300
So you basically worked a whole month for one phone and its plan.

18
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:04,400
Phone or plan, which one hurts more? Pick one.

